e Yellowstone, 308 feet, and the canyon below them \u2014 rhyolite cooked yellow and pink by the steam still rising from its walls."}, {"lat": 44.554, "lon": -110.4005, "d": 12, "az": 205, "el": 15, "cap": "Yellowstone Lake, 7,733 feet \u2014 the largest high lake on the continent. The Lake Hotel took its first guests in 1891, five years before this folio was printed."}, {"lat": 44.46, "lon": -110.8303, "d": 8, "az": 160, "el": 14, "cap": "The Upper Geyser Basin \u2014 Old Faithful and the greatest concentration of geysers on earth, the sinter aprons drawn white on Hague's sheet."}]}, {"id": "fire", "name": "Fire Below", "blurb": "The Firehole's chain of basins, south to north \u2014 every white patch on the sheet is hot ground.", "keys": [{"lat": 44.356, "lon": -110.797, "d": 8, "az": 250, "el": 14, "cap": "Shoshone Geyser Basin, reached only by trail then and now \u2014 Union Geyser and Minute Man steaming beside Shoshone Lake."}, {"lat": 44.46, "lon": -110.8303, "d": 6, "az": 210, "el": 13, "cap": "Geyser Hill: Old Faithful, Beehive, Giantess. Hague timed the eruptions; the intervals he printed still roughly hold."}, {"lat": 44.525, "lon": -110.838, "d": 6, "az": 180, "el": 13, "cap": "Midway \u2014 Excelsior's ragged crater, which threw water three hundred feet in the 1880s and wrecked itself doing it, beside the great blue lens of Grand Prismatic."}, {"lat": 44.5486, "lon": -110.8036, "d": 9, "az": 250, "el": 14, "cap": "The Lower Basin \u2014 Fountain Paint Pot, Great Fountain, White Dome \u2014 and the Fountain Hotel, whose guests walked out after dinner to watch the mud boil."}]}, {"id": "absaroka", "name": "The Absaroka Front", "blurb": "The park's eastern wall \u2014 Absaroka volcano ruins over the Lamar's bison ground.", "keys": [{"lat": 44.894, "lon": -110.221, "d": 14, "az": 120, "el": 15, "cap": "The Lamar Valley \u2014 the 'secluded valley' of the trappers, where the Army wintered the last wild bison herd in the 1890s."}, {"lat": 44.9422, "lon": -110.2078, "d": 9, "az": 30, "el": 15, "cap": "Barronette Peak over Soda Butte Creek, banded with the folio's purple-brown Absaroka breccias \u2014 the wreck of Eocene volcanoes."}, {"lat": 44.7975, "lon": -110.4339, "d": 10, "az": 90, "el": 16, "cap": "Mount Washburn, 10,243 feet \u2014 the stage road crossed its shoulder at Dunraven Pass; tourists climbed at dawn for the view across the whole sheet."}, {"lat": 44.47, "lon": -110.09, "d": 16, "az": 100, "el": 17, "cap": "The crest above the lake \u2014 Avalanche Peak and First Peoples Mountain, renamed in 2022 for those who knew this country ten thousand years before the Survey came."}]}, {"id": "caldera", "name": "The Great Caldera", "blurb": "Half the sheet is one orange colour. Hague mapped an immense rhyolite field; it took seventy more years to read the crater in it.", "keys": [{"lat": 44.6, "lon": -110.55, "d": 46, "az": 225, "el": 28, "cap": "Hague's rhyolite, edge to edge \u2014 one lava field the size of a county. Nobody in 1896 could see the 640,000-year-old caldera it fills; the reading came in the 1960s."}, {"lat": 44.416, "lon": -110.5732, "d": 9, "az": 245, "el": 14, "cap": "West Thumb \u2014 a crater within the crater: a younger, smaller explosion flooded by the lake, its bay floor still venting."}, {"lat": 44.275, "lon": -110.8, "d": 16, "az": 200, "el": 17, "cap": "Pitchstone Plateau \u2014 the youngest flow on the sheet, glassy rhyolite barely 70,000 years old, treeless down its spine."}, {"lat": 44.718, "lon": -110.493, "d": 9, "az": 20, "el": 15, "cap": "And where the river cuts the field: hydrothermally altered rhyolite in every shade of yellow \u2014 the wall that named the park."}]}], "mines": [{"n": "Amphitheater Springs", "u": 0.31626, "v": 0.23987, "c": "spring"}, {"n": "Apollinaris Spring", "u": 0.30932, "v": 0.19961, "c": "spring"}, {"n": "Artists Paintpots", "u": 0.30496, "v": 0.33171, "c": "spring"}, {"n": "Asta Spring", "u": 0.19545, "v": 0.52237, "c": "spring"}, {"n": "Beach Springs", "u"